# Conventions

## Context

The seed app enforces strict TypeScript usage and a handful of naming and
style conventions that keep the codebase consistent and easy to review.

## Rule (tied to files)

- app/src/actions.ts: use named exports only (no default exports); action
  creators must be exported as named functions.
- app/src/types.ts: types and interfaces must use PascalCase; avoid `any` and
  avoid `@ts-ignore` directives.
- app/src/reducer.ts: reducer updates must be immutable — use spread, map,
  filter; do not mutate objects/arrays in place (no push/splice/assign to
  state/task fields).
- Files should be kebab-case; other general conventions still apply.

## How to verify

1. Check `app/src/actions.ts` contains only named exports (no `export default`) and exports functions (e.g., `export function ...`).
2. Check `app/src/types.ts` for PascalCase type/interface names and that it does not contain `any` or `@ts-ignore`.
3. Check `app/src/reducer.ts` for immutable patterns (use of `map`, `filter`, spread) and absence of mutation operations such as `.push`, `.splice`, or direct assignments to `state.` or `task.` fields.
4. Run `npm run typecheck` in `app/` and expect no TypeScript errors.

(These checks are intentionally file-scoped so automated verifications can point
to a specific source file when rules are violated.)